  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 933 AM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nThe upper-level high will be stretching across the southern CONUS \ntoday, pushing mid-level dry air into our region from the east. \nBut even with this push of dry air, the combination of PWAT values\nranging between 1.1-1.3\" and the continuous stream of mid-level \nmoisture west of the Continental Divide will favor afternoon \nthunderstorm activity in the area mountains and western New \nMexico. The desert lowlands will only see a slight chance (10-20%)\nfor thunderstorm development as warmer air in the upper-level \nhigh will hinder the forcing needed for storm development. \nTemperatures today and for the rest of the week will run 4-7 \ndegrees above average.\n\nA passing upper-level trough in the northern Midwest will weaken and \neventually split the upper-level high in two on Wednesday, with\nthe western area of high pressure redeveloping over the desert\nsouthwest by Thursday. The lower edge of the trough will bring \nmoisture and lift to the region on Wednesday and Thursday, with \nthe best chance for scattered thunderstorms (30-60%) in Gila and \nLincoln National Forests. The lowlands are expected see a slight \nchance (10-20%) of afternoon/evening thunderstorms on Wednesday, \nand an isolated chance (20-40%) on Thursday. For both days, the \nbiggest risks with these storms are gusty outflow winds and \nlocalized flooding--especially in the mountains.\n\nStorm activity is expected to decrease on Friday as the trough\nmoves east, with storms limited to the mountains and far west New\nMexico. Friday and Saturday are also forecast to be the hottest \ndays of the week as the upper-level high persists, with the lower\nRio Grande Valley reaching the triple-digit mark both days. Some\npassing troughs over the Rocky Mountains will mostly dissolve the\nupper-level high early next week, and that should bring some\nbetter rain chances for the area on Sunday and Monday.",
